"Sammiel" <lowrysam@gmail.com> writes:

> As the subject asks, what information in the spoilers can or can't,
> even if only in principle, be discovered in-game?
> 
> Clearly some information is trivial: various uses of items, skills and
> intrinsics gained and lost due to various events, etc....
> 
> But some not so: what about probabilities? I would say that these in
> fact can be discovered. Given a large enough number of carefully
> recorded games, you could find out that gems do indeed comprise 8% of
> all randomly generated objects in the main dungeon. Your answer might
> only ever be approximate, but over time it would tend to the correct
> figure.
> 
> How would one go about determining the weights of items? It only takes
> a few games to notice that a zorkmid is the lightest object in the game
> (what else can you carry 90000 of?), so that weight would be your
> margin of error when determining how much an item weighs by seeing how
> many zorkmids it displaces to make you burdened again.
> 
> (It's more work to discover the effect of Str on carrying capacity, and
> maybe even a creative leap to realise that Con has an effect too.)

I've seen "You feel tough!  Your movements are now unencumbered" which
is a pretty good clue that Con affects carrying capacity.

> Discovering nutritional values for comestibles is harder. Again you
> have to build a nutrition-o-meter within the game. Perhaps if you could
> assume that praying for a contented stomach always resets
> ("calibrates") your nutrition status to a constant value, you could
> then count how many jackal corpses you were required to scoff down
> before becoming satiated. If experimentation revealed that this value
> was constant, you could be more confident in your assumption.

Very tricky, because there are several non-obvious factors which
affect food consumption.  Perhaps you could start by playing foodless
characters, note that you seem to take roughly the same time to become
hungry again each time your god fills your stomach, and work from
there to nail down the food consumption formula.  Once you have that,
just note how many extra turns each food item gives you.

> Monster "difficulty" is tough to discover. In principle, you just get a
> big stack of corpses and see how many each takes to reset your prayer
> timeout. In practice, arbitrary corpses are hard to generate on-demand,
> and the longer you wait between sacrifices, the more the intervening
> turns will have an effect on reducing timeout, thus increasing the
> error of your experiment.

Virtually impossible I'd say - prayer timeout is random.

> Luck would be almost impossible. How do you build a luck-o-meter? The
> principle is the same as for the above: find two situations which give
> you a known amount of luck, then measure how many repetitions of a
> certain action it takes to move from one luck-value to another. The
> trouble is how to accurately measure luck. Wands of enlightenment are
> rough tools. It would take an incredibly sophisticated experiment to
> discover that "Hurtling through the air in Sokoban due to Newton's 3rd
> Law" decreased your baseline luck by -1. Any budding scientists got any
> ideas?

Once you've enlightened yourself while carrying a luckstone, you'll
know that luck exists, and (by implication) that it normally times
out.  You might plausibly guess that sacrificing increases luck
(because of the four-leaf clovers) and go from there to discovering
that it takes a multiple of 600 turns to time out.  The problem would
be discovering which actions have any effect on luck.

> In asking whether this meta-game is always winnable, we return to the
> original question: what *can't* be discovered in-game? Something to do
> with magic cancellation perhaps? Full effects of AC? Exact mechanics of
> hit-point regeneration?

There's a rumour "An elven cloak protects against magic".  The alert
player might notice that when they wear an elven cloak, certain
attacks hardly ever work.

The mechanics of exercising stats and training pets would probably be
impossible to discover in game - how would you go beyond qualitative
observations like "pushing boulders is good for strength" and "feeding
tripe to a cat or dog makes it more inclined to fetch"?

The formula for donating to priests is often mentioned as an example
of something which would be difficult for an unspoiled player to
figure out.
